Z||000000||JohnF||09-08-2001||07:58 PM||||I am wondering how, exactly, to tell if new shocks/struts are warranted.  The old method of bouncing up and down seems to have been discredited for the Fiero.  Seems as if shocks/struts can give a correct response to this method because of springing and may not be an indicator of shock/strut condition.  I have no leaks and no indication of wear other than mileage.  What gives?||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||09-08-2001||08:16 PM||||How old are they? milage whatever?<P>If they aren't leaking then all you've got is age. <P>Warrantee? that all depends what shocks struts they are. That's not really relevant to wear unless you are going to replace them. <P>Shocks last on average several years. Struts usually last longer. Of course this all depends on how you drive and the roads you drive on.<P>If you still have OE struts at this point I'd replace them. Same for OE shocks. If they are newer it's a tougher call.<P>Shocks/struts tend to wear mainly in the center of their range, especially with stiff springs. The "bounce test" doesn't detect that kind of wear, even on other vehicles. It only detects if the shock has blown a valve internally thus no longer killing large throw movement. It can't detect the more subtle performance loss of piston/cylinder wear.||||reg||1
